2026-01-08 13:54:21 - claude-primitive-finder - INFO - Claude thinks: Let me understand the bins offset. The bins array starts after:- mutex (4 bytes aligned to 8)
- flags (4 bytes)
- have_fastchunks (4 bytes)- fastbinsY[10] for 64-bit (10 * 8 = 80 bytes)
- top (8 bytes)
- last_remainder (8 bytes)So the bins offset in malloc_state is approximately 8 + 4 + 4 + 80 + 8 + 8 = 112 bytes = 0x70

The unsorted bin is bins[0] which is at offset 0x70 in main_arena.

So if main_arena is at 0x1e7ac0:- bins[0] is at 0x1e7ac0 + 0x70 = 0x1e7b30 (approximately)

When a chunk is freed to the unsorted bin with no other chunks there, its fd/bk will point to &main_arena.bins[0] - 0x10 (because fd/bk are at chunk + 0x10, so the list head needs to be offset).

Actually, in glibc's bin implementation, the fd/bk of a free chunk point to the next/prev chunk in the bin. When the bin is empty or has one chunk, they point to the "bin head" which is part of the bins array. The bin head for unsorted bin is at bins[0]/bins[1].

